## Inserting and Removing by Position

While `push`/`pop` work on the end, `.insert()` and `.remove()` let you modify elements at any position.

### insert — Add at Index

```xiom
var v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(3); v.push(4);
v.insert(1, 2);  // insert 2 at index 1
// v: [1, 2, 3, 4]

v.insert(0, 0);  // insert 0 at beginning
// v: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]

v.insert(v.len(), 5);  // insert at end (like push)
// v: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### remove — Remove at Index

```xiom
var v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20); v.push(30); v.push(40);

let removed = v.remove(1);  // removes element at index 1 (20)
// v: [10, 30, 40]
// removed: 20
```

### Performance Note

`.insert()` and `.remove()` shift all subsequent elements, making them O(n). For frequent middle insertions, consider `LinkedList` instead.

### Practical Uses

```xiom
// Maintain sorted order
fn insert_sorted(v: &mut Vec[Int], value: Int) -> Unit {
  var i = 0;
  while i < v.len() && v[i] < value {
    i = i + 1;
  }
  v.insert(i, value);
}

// Remove all occurrences
fn remove_all(v: &mut Vec[Int], target: Int) -> Unit {
  var i = 0;
  while i < v.len() {
    if v[i] == target {
      v.remove(i);
      // don't increment i — next element shifted into position
    } else {
      i = i + 1;
    }
  }
}
```

### Insert/Remove Patterns

```xiom
// Insert at front (slow — O(n))
v.insert(0, value);

// Remove from front (slow — O(n))
v.remove(0);

// Insert at back (fast — O(1))
v.insert(v.len(), value);
// same as v.push(value);
```

